# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated data
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The built corpus. 13 MB gzipped, 67 MB raw, and fully reproducible from
# data/raw/ with `make corpus` in about seven seconds. Derived data does not
# belong in git when the inputs and the builder are both already there.
data/corpus/provisions.jsonl
data/corpus/provisions.jsonl.gz
data/corpus/provisions_permissive.jsonl
data/corpus/provisions_permissive.jsonl.gz
data/corpus/stats.json

# data/corpus/fact_patterns.json and data/seed/formalizations.jsonl are also
# generated -- by `make seed`, out of src/nomos/fact_patterns.py and the Lean
# corpus modules -- but they are small (10 KB and 83 KB) and they are the part
# of the data a reader actually wants to browse without installing a Lean
# toolchain first. They stay tracked on purpose. If you would rather they did
# not, uncomment:
#
# data/corpus/fact_patterns.json
# data/seed/formalizations.jsonl

# Fetched by `python -m nomos.fetch.restricted` on a machine with ordinary
# network access. Third-party text under assorted licences; keep it local.
data/raw/restricted/

# Pipeline and training outputs.
data/runs/
data/train/
checkpoints/
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.gguf
wandb/
runs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# data/raw/ is TRACKED on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ~62 MB of staged source texts (Latin Library, Sefaria, Daizhige). It is
# third-party and it is fetchable, so tracking it is a real choice: it makes
# the build hermetic and keeps the licence files next to the texts they cover,
# which for a project whose whole point is provenance is worth the megabytes.
#
# To go the other way -- lean repo, fetch on demand -- uncomment the block
# below and restore the sources with the git commands in DATA_LICENSES.md.
#
# data/raw/latin_library/
# data/raw/sefaria/
# data/raw/sinica/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notebooks
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*-checkpoint.ipynb

# Notebook OUTPUTS are committed on purpose. They are the fastest way to see
# what the corpus and the pipeline actually produce without running anything,
# and `make run-notebooks` regenerates them deterministically. If that churns
# your diffs, install nbstripout instead of ignoring the files.

